---
import { icon } from '../../lib/icons.js';
import DemonstrationCard from '../DemonstrationCard.astro';
import ExecutionDemo from '../ExecutionDemo.astro';
---

<section id="setup" class="section setup-showcase">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2>Benchmark composition and evaluation protocol</h2>
    <div class="report-prose">
      <p>
        The main evaluation covers the 26 EBench test-mini tasks in nine scene categories: 19 mobile
        and seven tabletop tasks, spanning household, retail, and industrial settings. Controlled
        variations change objects, backgrounds, instructions, and their combinations. We report{' '}
        <strong>equal-weight task means</strong> for complete success (SR) and normalized terminal
        partial credit (Score), using 510 retained episodes.
      </p>
      <h3>Agentic control and learned policies</h3>
      <p>
        GPT-6-Astra runs with <code>high</code> reasoning effort. It requests end-effector (EEF)
        poses, gripper and base targets, and action durations; a fixed execution interface carries
        out those requests.{' '}
        <strong>
          No learned vision-language-action (VLA) or world-action model (WAM) policy generates or
          refines its actions
        </strong>
        . <strong>The agent receives new observations when a tool call returns</strong>, after the
        whole waypoint sequence has run. The interface logs execution in chunks of up to eight
        physics steps; the model makes its next decision after the complete call.
      </p>
      <p>
        The VLA/WAM policies are trained on the publicly released EBench dataset, which contains{' '}
        <strong>6,600 episodes across 26 tasks</strong>. For task-specific adaptation, GPT-6-Astra
        receives <strong>one historical demonstration per task, without parameter updates</strong>.
        A separate GPT-6-Astra instance running with <code>high</code> reasoning effort reviews one
        video trajectory from the training set and{' '}
        <strong>autonomously selects the keyframes and writes the accompanying prompts</strong>. The
        resulting ICL package contains 10–18 annotated keyframes, operation phases, arm roles, and
        selected state/action examples. Every episode of a task uses the same package. The model
        adapts the procedure to live observations without task-specific parameter updates or
        coordinate replay.
      </p>
      <h3>Observations and evaluation conditions</h3>
      <p>
        GPT-6-Astra receives RGB observations from the{' '}
        <strong>overview, left wrist, and right wrist cameras</strong>. All three views are provided
        at the start of an episode; subsequent tool calls return the camera views requested by the
        model. The accompanying state includes{' '}
        <strong>
          end-effector poses, gripper state, base pose, and the current simulator timestep
        </strong>
        . Joint states are used by the execution interface and recorded in the logs. During
        execution, the model receives{' '}
        <strong>
          no depth data, ground-truth object poses, camera calibration, segmentation, intermediate
          task scores, or subgoal-completion signals
        </strong>
        . The simulator timestep indicates elapsed steps; the task’s maximum physics-step budget is
        not supplied to the model.
      </p>
      <p>
        We impose no additional aggregate cap on the model’s{' '}
        <strong>token budget, total tool calls or inference time</strong>. Physical execution
        follows the <strong>same task-specific step limits as the baseline policies</strong>.
        GPT-6-Astra may end its action sequence with a termination signal;{' '}
        <strong>the server determines task success and Score</strong>. All eight models are compared
        on the same task suite and evaluation metrics.
      </p>
      <h3>A separate test of unseen composition</h3>
      <p>
        The proof-of-concept (POC) experiment sits <strong>outside the 26-task cohort</strong>.
        Comparison policies are retrained on its atomic skills and familiar objects, but the
        evaluated task combinations are unseen.{' '}
        <strong>No ground-truth (GT) trajectories are available for the composed POC tasks</strong>,
        so all models use <strong>zero-shot execution</strong>. This tests whether they can
        understand and compose a new task from familiar skills.
      </p>
      <DemonstrationCard />
      <div class="method-details">
        <button class="appendix-link" data-appendix="protocol">
          Evaluation protocol <Fragment set:html={icon('external-link')} />
        </button>
        <a
          href="https://internrobotics.github.io/EBench-doc/getting-started/overview/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          Benchmark overview <Fragment set:html={icon('external-link')} />
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://internrobotics.github.io/EBench-doc/getting-started/overview/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          Evaluation metrics <Fragment set:html={icon('external-link')} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ExecutionDemo />
  </div>
</section>
